How to Test Each Connection in Your Network Lab
1. Test PC to Router Connectivity

Purpose: Confirm that your PC can communicate with the router.

Steps:

    On your PC:

        Go to Desktop > Command Prompt.

        Type:

ping <Router_Interface_IP>

Example:

        ping 192.169.2.1

        Press Enter.

Expected Result:
You should see replies, meaning PC and router are connected.
2. Test PC to HTTP Server

Purpose: Verify that the PC can reach the HTTP server by IP and get a webpage.

Steps:

    Ping the HTTP Server IP from PC:

        In Command Prompt:

    ping 192.169.2.2

    Press Enter.

Access HTTP Server via Browser:

    Go to Desktop > Web Browser on PC.

    Enter the HTTP server IP:

        http://192.169.2.2

        Press Enter.

Expected Result:
You should see the webpage hosted on the HTTP server.
3. Test PC to FTP Server

Purpose: Test file transfer connection from PC to FTP server.

Steps:

    On PC Command Prompt, type:

ftp 192.169.2.4

(Replace 192.169.2.4 with your FTP server IP.)

When prompted, enter the username and password you configured on the FTP server.

After login, test commands like:

ls

(to list files) or

    get filename

    (to download a file).

Expected Result:
You should successfully connect and interact with files on the FTP server.
4. Test DNS Resolution on PC

Purpose: Check if the PC can translate domain names (like your website name) into IP addresses using the DNS server.

Setup DNS on PC:

    On PC, go to Desktop > IP Configuration.

    Set the DNS Server IP to your DNS server’s static IP (example: 192.169.2.3).

Test DNS:

    On PC Command Prompt, type:

nslookup <website-name>

Example:

    nslookup mywebsite.com

    (The website name is the one you added in the DNS server configuration pointing to the HTTP server IP.)

Expected Result:
You should see the DNS server respond with the IP address of the HTTP server.
5. Test HTTP Server Using Domain Name (via DNS)

Purpose: Access the HTTP server using the domain name resolved by DNS.

Steps:

    On PC’s web browser, type:

    http://mywebsite.com

    (Replace mywebsite.com with the domain name configured in your DNS server.)

Expected Result:
The browser loads the webpage hosted on the HTTP server, confirming that DNS resolution and HTTP communication are working.
